When a C++ application initialize log level using `FC_LOG_LEVEL_INIT`, it will retrieve an integer reference to the log level stored in Console. `setLogLevel` let user dynamically change that integer log level. Various log printing macros, `FC_TRACE, FC_LOG, FC_MSG`, etc will check for the log level, and only output log if the log level integer value is equal or greater than its corresponding level. The end result is that, for example, when you issue `App.setLogLevel("my Workbench", "Error")` in python console, any log printing (with tag 'my Workbench') NOT using `FC_ERR` will be silenced.  If you issue `App.setLogLevel("my Workbench", "Message")`, then log output with `FC_ERR, FC_WARN, FC_MSG` will show, and the others silenced.

The most obvious use case is that if a user encounters a problem, he can now have more informal reports by turning up the log level and attach the report with log output in the python console.

There is also `App.getLogLevel`, so python code that needs logging can actually do similar thing. 
```python
import FreeCAD
import FreeCADGui

class FCADLogger:
    def __init__(self, tag):
        self.tag = tag

    def isEnabledFor(self,level):
        return FreeCAD.getLogLevel(self.tag) >= level

    def trace(self,msg):
        if self.isEnabledFor(4):
            FreeCAD.Console.PrintLog(msg+'\n')
            self.updateGui()

    def log(self,msg):
        if self.isEnabledFor(3):
            FreeCAD.Console.PrintLog(msg+'\n')
            self.updateGui()

    def message(self,msg):
        if self.isEnabledFor(2):
            FreeCAD.Console.PrintMessage(msg+'\n')
            self.updateGui()

    def warning(self,msg):
        if self.isEnabledFor(1):
            FreeCAD.Console.PrintWarning(msg+'\n')
            self.updateGui()

    def error(self,msg):
        if self.isEnabledFor(0):
            FreeCAD.Console.PrintError(msg+'\n')
            self.updateGui()

    def updateGui(self):
        try:
            FreeCADGui.updateGui()
        except Exception:
            pass

logger = FCADLogger('myTag')

# either show or not depending on last setLogLevel
logger.trace('you see me?')

FreeCAD.setLogLevel('myTag','Trace')

# will show
logger.trace('now you see me')

FreeCAD.setLogLevel('myTag','Message')

# won't show
logger.trace('bye bye')
```
The actual log level is stored in `Parameters->BaseApp->LogLevels`. Edit those parameters manually will only take effect on restart. Use `App.setLogLevel` instead.

