> But I still couldn't find which part of that function is produing a bytestring...

Isn't that obvious? The line "if not isinstance(HTML, bytes):" is passed if the string is a unicode/str object and will be encoded to return a bytes object. If the string is already a bytes object nothing is done. Since there exists no further string type in Python it's always a bytes object, then.

> So this means that my changes now should break the py2-tests.

Obviously not. When using "ss" then it also works when passing a unicode object but only as long as the encoding to ASCII is possible. When passing some Chinese characters instead then it fails.
Now with PyArg_ParseTuple it's also possible to pass arbitrary unicode strings but we must tell it explicitly to use the utf-8 encoding then. The call must be of this form:
`
    char* fn;
    if (!PyArg_ParseTuple(args, "et", "utf-8", &fn))
        return NULL;
   
    std::string utf8Name = fn;
    PyMem_Free(fn);
`
So, I think your PR can be merged now. But then we have to adjust BrowserViewPy::setHtml to support unicode and then it would be good to write a unit test.

