I use gcc 9.2.0 (it comes with Arch Linux). redundant-move warning was introduced with version 9, you can see some details on this page : https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc-9.1.0/gcc/C_002b_002b-Dialect-Options.html (search for -Wno-redundant-move) and it DOES include the case when the return expression type is convertible to the return type, like below (I copy from the previous URL):
```
struct T { // convertible to U
…
};
struct U {
…
};
U fn()
{
  T t;
  …
  return std::move (t); // WARNING
}

```
It is the implementation of the Core Issue 1579 (http://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/cwg_defects.html#1579).

But I understand older versions of gcc and clang don't see things the same way, and all of that may seem quite abstruse nitpicks about warnings, so please feel free to reject my PR!

However, if you are interested in suppressing the warning for both older and newer versions of compilers, maybe other ways to solve it could be investigated:
- explicitly build an object of Base type from the Derived rvalue (via a move constructor), and return that object (rather than include an implicit conversion in the return statement)?
- avoid the use of Derived class in the functions (use only the Base class)?
I don't if the above suggestions are realistic and efficient so, once again, please feel free to reject the PR!

