> I don't understand what you mean that the problem is not solved. The problem is solved with these changes as far a I can tell.

There are two problems:
The problem that the preview disappears when selecting a reference axis is solved.

If you watch the video carefully there is an example where a circle and a cylinder is given. As soon as you select the circle and create an additive helix both objects disappear and the helix re-appears as soon as you select the correct reference. This is because in this case  the default vertical axis causes the helix feature to fail.

It only happens for an additive but not subtractive helix and the reason is the following:
When adding a new (solid) object to a body it will hide all previous solids, i.e. the cylinder in this case.
Then the helix will enter edit mode and this is how it's implemented (which is totally correct):
```
bool ViewProviderHelix::setEdit(int ModNum)
{

    if (ModNum == ViewProvider::Default ) {
        auto* prim = static_cast<PartDesign::Helix*>(getObject());
        setPreviewDisplayMode(prim->getAddSubType() == PartDesign::FeatureAddSub::Subtractive);
    }
    return ViewProviderAddSub::setEdit(ModNum);
}
```

The function setPreviewDisplayMode() then makes the base object (i.e. the cylinder) tmp. visible again -- but only for a subtractive helix. The cylinder for an additive helix stays invisible.

A solution to this problem can be that when creating an additive helix we first assign the shape of the base object to the helix because then it has something to show in case its initial recompute fails.

This second issue is independent of this PR but I mentioned it because I saw it in the video when I tried to understand the problematic. In other words the PR can be merged now.

