Parts of it but not everything, e.g. the std::find stuff. In general using std::find has some advantages over using a for-loop:
* for a reviewer it's immediately clear what the intention is while with a for-loop its body must be studied first (Not a big improvement in this case)
* the code will be smaller (Not a big improvement in this case)
* it's faster and safer

If you use a for-loop like this:
`for (it = ObjectsListVisible.begin(); it != ObjectsListVisible.end(); ++it) {`
then for every iteration step it must evaluate ObjectsListVisible.end(). Additionally it can happen that you unintentionally remove an element from the vector while iterating it and this invalidates the iterator it and thus will cause a crash for the next iteration step.
Now have a look at https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/algorithm/find where you can find its implementation:
```
template<class InputIt, class T>
constexpr InputIt find(InputIt first, InputIt last, const T& value)
{
    for (; first != last; ++first) {
        if (*first == value) {
            return first;
        }
    }
    return last;
}
```
In the for-loop it uses first != last and thus doesn't have to create a tmp. iterator object like it does with ObjectsListVisible.end().

Btw, when changing ObjectsListVisible to use DocumentObjectT you cannot use std::find any more but you have to use std::find_if instead. Your code will then look like:
```
        App::DocumentObject* anObject = doc->getObject(CutZName);
        std::vector<App::DocumentObjectT> ObjectsListVisible;
        auto it = std::find_if(ObjectsListVisible.begin(), ObjectsListVisible.end(), [anObject](const App::DocumentObjectT& obj) {
            return (obj.getObject() == anObject);
        });
        if (it != ObjectsListVisible.end()) {
            ObjectsListVisible.erase(it);
        }
```

