I have not yet tested the PR and thus don't know in how far it avoids the crash but it doesn't look like it addresses the actual reason of the crash.

According to the debugger output the crash happens in DynamicProperty::removeDynamicProperty() at line 247 `index.erase(it);`
For testing purposes I created a PD box and a Part cylinder where for one face of the box a sub-shape binder was created.

When following the instructions of procedure A the sub-shape binder is set to a face of the cylinder and clicking OK triggers SubShapeBinder::onChanged(). After several function calls it invokes DynamicProperty::removeDynamicProperty() where name is "Cache_Box". The property to be removed is a App::PropertyMatrix.

In line 245 the call of `GetApplication().signalRemoveDynamicProperty(*prop);` again triggers SubShapeBinder::onChanged() that again invokes DynamicProperty::removeDynamicProperty() with the same value of name, i.e. "Cache_Box".

So, this is a recursive call of DynamicProperty::removeDynamicProperty() and the removal of the iterator in the second call invalidates the iterator object of the first call and thus the repeated call of `index.erase(it);` leads to the crash.

Changing the corresponding part in DynamicProperty::removeDynamicProperty() fixes the crash:
```
        // Handle possible recursive calls of removeDynamicProperty
        if (prop->myName) {
            Property::destroy(prop);
            index.erase(it);
            // memory of myName has been freed
            prop->myName = nullptr;
        }
```

In case it can happen that there is a recursive call of DynamicProperty::removeDynamicProperty() for different properties then a few more things must be done:
* save the argument name to a local std::string because the address of name might be equal to prop->myName and use the std::string for the search
* before erasing the iterator perform a new search in index

