Ok I found how to fix the issue ! @abdullahtahiriyo I'm sure you're going to love this hacky hack! :D 
in `on_listWidgetElements_itemPressed`

Add `item->setSelected(false);`
```
            // first update the listwidget. Item is selected if at least one element of the geo is selected.
            item->setSelected(false);
            item->setSelected(item->isLineSelected || item->isStartingPointSelected || item->isEndPointSelected || item->isMidPointSelected);
```
It appears that calling setSelected(true), if the state was already true (ie item was already selected) then the bug occurs. Probably because setSelected returns if state don't change. And setSelected probably calls something important.
This fix is probably dirty, but it does solve the bug. Maybe we can already merge this to solve the issue for now before finding a better solution? Or maybe this gives you enough clue to find root issue?

The hack is already what is used in "onSelectionChanged".

If I remember correctly, we decided to switch from handling the signal of selection change to item pressed, because selection change would not be triggered when clicking on an already clicked item. Now, it appears we miss it so much that we are triggering the selection change on the parent to complete for what item pressed is not giving us. Probably, a cleaner solution would have been to handle both slots, using itemPressed to decide whether a selection change should be triggered, and handling selection changes via the appropriate signal (unless there is a better way via the selection model that we have been unable to find).

Anyway, to avoid back a forth and retests and fix the bug, yes, I think we can go  with something in the line of:
```
            // first update the listwidget. Item is selected if at least one element of the geo is selected.
            bool selected = item->isLineSelected || item->isStartingPointSelected || item->isEndPointSelected || item->isMidPointSelected;

            {
                QSignalBlocker sigblk(ui->listWidgetElements);

                if(item->isSelected() && selected) {
                    item->setSelected(false);// if already selected and changing or adding subelement, ensure selection change is triggered, which ensures timely repaint
                    item->setSelected(selected);
                }
                else {
                    item->setSelected(selected);
                }
            }
```

This will just cause more repaints, which is not optimal, but it is a minimal fix for the bug.

