Yes, indeed, that is what I meant, minimal distance in the common perpendicular, i.e. in the line through both centers.

The intersection between circles is not interesting (sometimes there is none). The points where this common perpendicular intersects both circles is the important bit. Because the circles can be located in any combination relative to each other, and the radii can be arbitrary too. I think it is the only way to know which point of each of the circles is to be considered for the distance.

