This does not appear to be what actually happens. I've extracted your matching algorithm to its own function for testing:
```
bool IndexedName::originalNameMatchAlgorithm(int i, std::vector<const char *> types, const char *name)
{
    for (const char * type : types) {
        int j=0;
        for (const char *n=name, *t=type; *n; ++n) {
            if (*n != *t || j >= i)
                break;
            ++j;
            ++t;
            if (!*t) {
                return true;
            }
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```
(it's identical to your original code, except it returns true or false instead of directly setting the name variable). Given the following test:
```
TEST_F(IndexedNameTest, originalNameMatchAlgorithm)
{
    const char* testName = "EDGE19";
    bool nameMatched = Data::IndexedName::originalNameMatchAlgorithm(4, allowedTypes, testName);
    EXPECT_TRUE(nameMatched);
    testName = "EDGES_ARE_REALLY_GREAT19";
    nameMatched = Data::IndexedName::originalNameMatchAlgorithm(22, allowedTypes, testName);
    EXPECT_FALSE(nameMatched);
}
```
This test fails, because `nameMatched` is `true` in both cases. As far as I can tell the algorithm you wrote is a "starts_with" algorithm, not a "strings are equal" algorithm. So if the requested name **starts with** any of the strings in the vector, the value from the vector is used, and the remainder of the name is discarded. If the intention is to actually ensure that the _entire_ name (minus its index) matches, we should just be using `std::strncmp`, rather than doing a manual letter-by-letter comparison.

I have tested this PR on Windows and I neither get a linking error with MSVC nor MSYS2. Also when removing the template instantiation block from Observer.h doesn't cause a failure for me.

But with RT's implementation I realized a problem:
```
#if defined (FC_OS_WIN32) || defined(FC_OS_CYGWIN)
#   ifdef FCBase
template class BaseExport Observer<const char*>;
template class BaseExport Subject<const char*>;
#   else
extern template class BaseExport Observer<const char*>;
extern template class BaseExport Subject<const char*>;
#   endif
#endif
```
For every translation unit of FreeCADBase that includes Observer.h the symbols are recreated by the compiler (confirmed with MSYS2). To avoid this the explicit template instantiation must move to Observer.cpp and the code in Observer.h should become:
```
extern template class BaseExport Observer<const char*>;
extern template class BaseExport Subject<const char*>;
``` 
This works fine with MSYS2 but now fails with MSVC when building FreeCADBase because the compiler dislikes the export macro. So, a workaround that works with MSVC and MSYS2 is
``` 
// Workaround for MSVC
#ifdef FreeCADBase_EXPORTS
#  define Base_EXPORT
#else
#  define Base_EXPORT  BaseExport
#endif

extern template class Base_EXPORT Observer<const char*>;
extern template class Base_EXPORT Subject<const char*>;
``` 
With MSYS2 I have confirmed that exclusively for Observer.cpp the symbols are defined by the compiler.

